                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "37a50335-b543-3672-8172-f600b922a2bf",
                        "entry_id": "1bfcecd0-09cd-3cc3-ab72-d8cceca8bc67",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Vocabulary; the totality of words of a language or a person."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1107d0a4-ab46-4cca-8c1f-6107d686c821",
                                "sense_id": "37a50335-b543-3672-8172-f600b922a2bf",
                                "text": "Wordiness is not usually considered a virtue any more than impurity is; but words are the wealth of English, and the riches of its word-hoard are only paralleled by the riches of the Anglo-American nations."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "246c5f35-853c-4a93-aca5-9d8abe245c39",
                                "sense_id": "37a50335-b543-3672-8172-f600b922a2bf",
                                "text": "Emerson came, unlocking his word hoard on nature and friendship and compensation, and irradiating his thoughts'with that peculiar slow smile that was like the emerging of the sun from behind a cloud."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b8cf5c2e-0deb-4db0-9e01-538feebf22c8",
                                "sense_id": "37a50335-b543-3672-8172-f600b922a2bf",
                                "text": "Beowulf unlocked his word hoard. “We come, indeed, from across the sea.”"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "59c395db-dee3-4d6d-9b2d-335447e3a535",
                                "sense_id": "37a50335-b543-3672-8172-f600b922a2bf",
                                "text": "Primary additions to the wordhoard tend to come from commercial sources[.]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ed68806b-771a-4903-b9c3-c123ae925ec3",
                                "sense_id": "37a50335-b543-3672-8172-f600b922a2bf",
                                "text": "Still, in spite of the sensation of being caught between a ‘shield-wall’ of opaque references and a ‘word-hoard’ that is old and strange, such readers are also bound to feel a certain ‘shock of the new’."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
